fs: statx: report a synthetic STATX_MNT_ID so modern flatpak works on 5.4

Every Flatpak app refuses to launch on this device with:

    error: statx didn't return all required fields: No data available

Root-caused 2026-08-03. Flatpak (via libglnx/ostree) calls

    statx(fd, "", AT_EMPTY_PATH,
          STATX_TYPE|STATX_INO|STATX_MNT_ID|STATX_MNT_ID_UNIQUE, &stx)

and then requires the mount-id bits to come back set in stx.stx_mask. Those
fields were added upstream in 5.8 (STATX_MNT_ID) and 6.8
(STATX_MNT_ID_UNIQUE); this 5.4 vendor kernel returns stx_mask=STATX_ALL
without them, so libglnx treats it as a hard failure and no app starts.

A userspace LD_PRELOAD shim does NOT fix this: libglnx issues statx via the
raw syscall, not the libc wrapper, so the interposed statx() is never
called (verified: the shim corrects a libc-wrapper statx but not flatpak's).
The fix therefore has to be in the kernel, in the one place every caller
goes through - cp_statx().

WHAT THIS DOES: report a mount id synthesised from the file's device number,
and set the STATX_MNT_ID / STATX_MNT_ID_UNIQUE bits. flatpak only uses the
mount id to decide whether two paths share a mount, and two paths on one
filesystem do, so a per-device id is correct for that use. This is the exact
value the (rejected) userspace shim used, which was confirmed to satisfy the
libglnx check via the libc path.

The value lands in __spare2[0], which is the slot upstream 5.8 repurposed as
stx_mnt_id, so the on-the-wire layout matches what userspace expects. No
struct or uapi change is needed; only cp_statx writes two extra words.

Not a real mount id (this kernel has no per-mount id infrastructure), but
correct for flatpak and harmless to anything that ignores the bits. Drop
this when moving to a mainline kernel, which reports a real one.

--- a/fs/stat.c
+++ b/fs/stat.c
@@ -551,4 +551,17 @@
 	tmp.stx_dev_major = MAJOR(stat->dev);
 	tmp.stx_dev_minor = MINOR(stat->dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * Backport of STATX_MNT_ID (5.8) / STATX_MNT_ID_UNIQUE (6.8): modern
+	 * flatpak/ostree hard-require these and refuse to run without them.
+	 * This kernel has no per-mount id, so synthesise a stable one from
+	 * the device number - flatpak only uses it to test whether two paths
+	 * share a mount, and same-device is a correct proxy. __spare2[0] is
+	 * the slot upstream 5.8 turned into stx_mnt_id, so userspace reads it
+	 * correctly. STATX_MNT_ID=0x1000, STATX_MNT_ID_UNIQUE=0x4000 (neither
+	 * is in this kernel's headers).
+	 */
+	tmp.__spare2[0] = ((__u64)MAJOR(stat->dev) << 32) | MINOR(stat->dev);
+	tmp.stx_mask |= 0x00001000U | 0x00004000U;
 
 	return copy_to_user(buffer, &tmp, sizeof(tmp)) ? -EFAULT : 0;
